# TLS termination and the only publicly reachable port.
#
# The app container does not publish a port of its own (see docker-compose.yml);
# it is reachable only on the compose network, as `app`. Everything from the
# outside arrives here.
#
# ## Why this file exists at all
#
# `AUTH_URL` is an https: origin in production, so Auth.js issues session
# cookies with the `__Secure-` prefix. A browser will not send those over plain
# HTTP. Serving the app directly on :3000 therefore produces a sign-in that
# completes, redirects, and lands you signed out — a TLS problem wearing a
# session bug's clothes. There is no configuration of the app that fixes it;
# the origin has to actually be https.
#
# ## What is deliberately not here
#
# No security headers. `next.config.ts` owns the CSP, HSTS, frame options and
# the rest, and `e2e/builder.spec.ts` asserts the exact directive list against
# the app. Setting any of them here too would mean two sources of truth for a
# value the test suite pins to one of them.
#
# ## Certificates
#
# Caddy obtains and renews Let's Encrypt certificates automatically, given
# only the hostname and reachable ports 80 and 443. They live in the
# `caddy_data` volume — without it every redeploy re-requests from Let's
# Encrypt and eventually hits the rate limit for the domain.

{$SITE_DOMAIN} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# X-Forwarded-For, -Proto and -Host are set by Caddy here. The first is
	# what `clientIp()` in src/server/request.ts reads for rate limiting; the
	# second is how Next knows the request was https despite arriving on
	# plain HTTP inside the compose network.
	reverse_proxy app:3000
}
